    historyContent: {
      origins: "Originating in the AustrondHungarian Empire, the name 'Ferbli' (or Färbeln) is derived from the German word 'Farbe' (suit). The earliest recorded prohibition of the game dates back to 1714, suggesting it was already a significant gambling game by the early 18th century.",
      originCountries: [
        'Hungary',
      ],
      timeline: [
        '1714: First historical reference in German gaming prohibitions.',
        '1800s: Becomes a social sensation in Hungarian high society and urban cafes.',
        'Early 20th Century: Replaced by Poker in many cities but preserved in rural areas.',
        'Present: Still played in specialized circles in Hungary and Transylvania.',
      ],
      evolution: 'The game moved from a three-card variant (Färbeln) to a four-card variant (Ferbli), introducing more complex betting patterns and wild card variations.',
      cultural: 'In Hungarian literature and cinema, Ferbli is often depicted as the quintessential game of rural gentry and high-stakes social competition.',
    },
